        "title": "Ship Structures",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "A ship hull is, structurally, a giant hollow box-section beam — a **box girder** —\nfloating on a support (buoyancy) that is distributed unevenly along its length and that\nchanges second by second as waves pass. The structural problem is to make this beam strong\nenough to resist bending, shear, and local pressure for 25 years of corrosion and millions\nof wave-induced load cycles, while being as light as possible (every tonne of steel is a\ntonne of lost cargo). This guide applies the beam mechanics of `structural/` to the\nspecific, hardest case: a beam that is also the buoyant body it must support."
    },
    {
