        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Ship Structures",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "The three stresses SUPERPOSE. A spot on the deck carries primary\n   (hull bending) + secondary (panel bending) + tertiary (plate bending)\n   stress at once. The sum must stay below yield with a margin.\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "04-SHIP-STRUCTURES.md#028",
      "path": [
        "04-SHIP-STRUCTURES.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:naval-architecture:04-ship-structures, git-history:naval-architecture:04-ship-structures]",
        "canonical_path": "naval-architecture/04-SHIP-STRUCTURES.md",
        "concepts": "[hull girder, longitudinal strength, hogging, sagging, scantlings, marine materials]",
        "current_path": "naval-architecture/04-SHIP-STRUCTURES.md",
        "id": "maxim:naval-architecture:ship-structures",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "naval-architecture",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[ship structures]",
        "section": "naval-architecture",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Ship Structures",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "This superposition is why a ship can be globally fine yet crack locally: the tertiary\nstress at a weld toe, riding on top of the primary deck stress, is where fatigue cracks\nnucleate. The neutral-axis material that does nothing for primary strength may still be\nworking hard at the secondary or tertiary scale."
    },
    {
      "id": "04-SHIP-STRUCTURES.md#029",
      "path": [
        "04-SHIP-STRUCTURES.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:naval-architecture:04-ship-structures, git-history:naval-architecture:04-ship-structures]",
        "canonical_path": "naval-architecture/04-SHIP-STRUCTURES.md",
        "concepts": "[hull girder, longitudinal strength, hogging, sagging, scantlings, marine materials]",
        "current_path": "naval-architecture/04-SHIP-STRUCTURES.md",
        "id": "maxim:naval-architecture:ship-structures",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "naval-architecture",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[ship structures]",
        "section": "naval-architecture",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Ship Structures",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "> Old world -> new world bridge. The three scales are a structural analog of separation of\n> concerns: a global invariant (the hull girder must not break) plus local invariants\n> (each panel, each plate) that must each hold, with their stresses *composing* rather than\n> being independent. Verifying the system means checking every level and their sum — you\n> cannot prove the whole safe by proving only the global property, the same way passing an\n> integration test does not discharge the unit-level obligations."
    },
