        "root_concepts": "[ship structures]",
        "section": "naval-architecture",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Ship Structures",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Steel is roughly as strong in tension as compression at the *material* level, but a thin\nplate or panel in compression fails by **buckling** — sideways collapse — long before it\nreaches yield stress. Because the deck is in compression during sagging and the bottom\nduring hogging, buckling, not yield, is frequently the governing failure mode for the hull\ngirder."
    },
